# iwconfig — real-world examples

> Legacy wireless tool — modern replacement is `iw` (and `nmcli` for NetworkManager). On Tumbleweed: `sudo zypper install wireless-tools` for iwconfig, `iw` is usually already there.

## Display wireless settings of an adapter

```sh
iwconfig wlan0
```

## Turn transmit power on / auto / off

```sh
iwconfig wlan0 txpower on
iwconfig wlan0 txpower auto
iwconfig wlan0 txpower off
```

## Change the mode of the wireless adapter

```sh
# Must usually bring the interface down first
iwconfig wlan0 mode managed
iwconfig wlan0 mode ad-hoc
iwconfig wlan0 mode monitor
```

## Homelab: check link quality on the laptop from across the room

```sh
watch -n1 'iwconfig wlan0 | grep -i quality'
```

## Homelab: modern equivalents with iw / nmcli

```sh
iw dev wlan0 info        # replaces: iwconfig wlan0
iw dev wlan0 link        # signal, bitrate for the current AP
nmcli device wifi list   # scan for networks via NetworkManager
```

| Mode    | What it does                              |
| ------- | ----------------------------------------- |
| managed | normal client of an access point          |
| ad-hoc  | peer-to-peer, no AP                       |
| monitor | passively capture all frames (sniffing)   |
